# iFive mini 4S — Android 16 GSI release {{DATE}}

- System: {{SYSTEM}}, in `images/super.img` together with the ASUS Tinker Board Android 11 vendor partition carrying the repository's `vendor-changes/`.
- Kernel: Linux 4.19, release config.
- Boot partition (`images/ramdisk.img`): {{BOOT}}.
- Partition map: system 3072 MiB @ 296 MiB, metadata 16 MiB @ 3368 MiB, userdata @ 3448 MiB.

## Contents
- `images/`: `resource.img` (device tree + logos), `kernel.img`, `ramdisk.img`, `super.img`, `metadata.img`, `userdata-head-zero.img`
- `loader/RK3288UbootLoader_V2.30.10.bin`; `loader/rk3288_loader_v1.12.263.bin` (the image `rkdeveloptool db` loads in Maskrom mode)
- `devices/<board>/`: per-board rescue data (parameter structures and the first 4/8 MiB of the eMMC)
- `flash.sh`, `restore.sh`, `write-loader-head.sh`, `lib-wl.inc`, `manifest.json` (write map), `tools/rkdeveloptool`
- `SHA256SUMS`, `BUILD-INFO.txt`

All write addresses come from `manifest.json`, which the build derives from the partition table in `images/resource.img` and the parameter structure of each board.

## Flashing
Needs Linux x86_64 with libusb-1.0. `tools/rkdeveloptool` is used; set `RKDEVELOPTOOL` to use another build.

1. Put the tablet in Rockchip download mode (USB `2207:320a`): power it off, hold Volume Down and plug in the USB cable. `./tools/rkdeveloptool ld` prints `Loader` or `Maskrom`.
2. Run:
   ```sh
   ./flash.sh --dry-run                 # print every command
   ./flash.sh --yes                     # replace everything, wipe /data
   ./flash.sh --yes --keep-userdata     # replace everything except metadata and the start of userdata
   ```
3. Each image is checked against `SHA256SUMS`, written in pieces, read back and compared. An interrupted flash continues where it stopped (`--fresh` starts over). The script clears the download-mode command in `misc` and resets the tablet. The first boot takes several minutes.

`--keep-userdata` keeps `/data` readable because `/data` on this tablet is plain ext4 on `mmcblk0p13` (`ro.crypto.state=unsupported`).

## Restoring a wiped tablet
`restore.sh` restores a tablet whose bootloader is intact but whose parameter structures (partition table) are erased. The tablet then still enumerates in Loader mode.

```sh
./restore.sh --plan              # print the write map
./restore.sh                     # detect the board, write parameters and all images
./restore.sh --board ifive-mini4s
./restore.sh --parameters-only   # write only the two parameter structures
./restore.sh --maskrom           # tablet in Maskrom mode: load the loader into RAM first
```

The board is detected by comparing the parameter structure at tool LBA 0x2000 with `devices/*/parameter-p1.img`; on a tablet with no parameter structure pass `--board`. The script asks for the word `restore` before writing (`--yes` skips that), reads every write back and stops on a mismatch. Reset the tablet with `tools/rkdeveloptool rd` when it prints `RESTORE COMPLETE`.

Write map for `ifive-mini4s`:

|what|tool LBA|raw sector|MiB|
|---|---|---|---|
|`devices/ifive-mini4s/parameter-loader.img`|0x0000|0x02000|4|
|`devices/ifive-mini4s/parameter-p1.img`|0x2000|0x04000|8|
|`images/resource.img`|0x6000|0x08000|16|
|`images/kernel.img`|0xE000|0x10000|32|
|`images/ramdisk.img`|0x16000|0x18000|48|
|`images/super.img`|0x92000|0x94000|296|
|`images/metadata.img`|0x692000|0x694000|3368|
|`images/userdata-head-zero.img`|0x6BA000|0x6BC000|3448|

`rkdeveloptool` counts from eMMC sector 0x2000: `tool LBA = raw sector - 0x2000`. The loader area (raw sectors 0x0000..0x1FFF: idblock at 0x40, `u-boot.itb` at 0x44) therefore has no tool LBA.

The partition table in the device tree gives `system` 3072 MiB, the stock parameter structure 1536 MiB; every partition behind `system` moves out by the difference (0x300000 sectors). `restore.sh` computes that difference from the two tables, checks every other partition against it, and stops before writing if any partition disagrees.

## A tablet with a blank eMMC
With no bootloader on the eMMC the tablet enumerates in Maskrom mode. In this order:

1. `./restore.sh --board ifive-mini4s --maskrom --parameters-only`: loads `loader/rk3288_loader_v1.12.263.bin` into RAM (Loader mode) and writes the parameter structures. RKDevTool/AFPTool on Windows (Download Boot with the same file) also gets the tablet into Loader mode.
2. `./write-loader-head.sh --board ifive-mini4s --lba-base tool`: writes `devices/ifive-mini4s/head-4M.bin` at raw sector 0, reads it back and checks the `RK32` tag at byte 0x8800. RKDevTool/AFPTool can write the same file at address 0.
3. `./restore.sh --board ifive-mini4s`: all images.

`--lba-base tool` is the numbering of the bundled `rkdeveloptool` (tool LBA 0 = raw sector 0x2000). Without `--lba-base`, `write-loader-head.sh` finds the numbering by reading the loader head back at both candidate addresses.

## Adding a board
Add `devices/<board>/device.json` plus `parameter-p1.img` (the 4 MiB p1 partition with 4 PARM structures at 0, 1, 2 and 3 MiB) and, from the first 8 MiB of that board's eMMC, `head-4M.bin`, `head-8M.bin` and `parameter-loader.img` (bytes 4..8 MiB, 8 PARM structures). `device.json` holds `board`, `display_name`, `stock_backup_dir`, `p1_parameter_gz`, `bootloader_8m_gz`, `parameter_region_lba`, `p1_lba`, `loader_area_note` and the sha256 of each file. The build checks the sizes, the PARM count, the `RK32` tag and the partition rule above.

## Magisk root
When the Boot partition line above names Magisk, `flash.sh` already writes the Magisk boot. Otherwise write the Magisk boot image published next to the zip (Magisk 31.0) after flashing:

```sh
sha256sum -c ifive-mini4s-android16-boot-magisk-v31.img.sha256
./tools/rkdeveloptool wl 0x16000 ../ifive-mini4s-android16-boot-magisk-v31.img
./tools/rkdeveloptool rd
```

Then install the Magisk 31.0 app. If it shows `Not installed` after the first boot, run from the repository (needs `adb root`) and reboot:

```sh
scripts/magisk-install-data.sh Magisk-v31.0.apk
```

It fills `/data/adb/magisk` the way Magisk's flashable-zip installer does. The app's own install step re-patches the boot image, and `magiskboot` cannot unpack this tablet's boot partition, which is a Rockchip `KRNL` container around a gzip cpio ramdisk. `scripts/make-magisk-boot.sh` builds the image with the ramdisk steps of Magisk's `boot_patch.sh`, adds `PREINITDEVICE=mmcblk0p13` to the Magisk config and repacks it with `tools/mkkrnl.py`. `flash.sh` writes the generic boot partition back.

## Device identity
`build.prop` in the system and vendor images names the tablet: brand `iFive`, model `iFive mini 4S`, name `ifive_mini4s`, device `mini4s`, and a matching `ro.build.fingerprint`. `ro.vendor.build.fingerprint` stays the Tinker Board Android 11 string of the vendor blobs. `scripts/patch-identity.sh` writes these keys; `ID_BRAND`, `ID_MANUFACTURER`, `ID_MODEL`, `ID_NAME`, `ID_DEVICE` change them.

## Memory clock (DDR)
- The loader starts the LPDDR2 at 396 MHz. The kernel's DDR frequency driver (`CONFIG_ARM_RK3288_DMC_DEVFREQ`, ported from the ChromeOS 4.19 RK3288 DMC, node `dmc-cros@ff610000`) runs on the devfreq `userspace` governor.
- `/vendor/etc/init/hw/init.rk3288.rc` sets 456 MHz (the stock firmware's normal rate, vdd_logic 1.15 V) once boot completes. Notification-shade janky frames, 16 animations per arm: 42.0% and 43.3% at 396 MHz, 33.9% and 34.5% at 456 MHz.
- The switch can corrupt the top lines of one frame, once per boot: the panel's vertical blank (194 µs) is shorter than a rate switch.
- Back to 396 MHz until the next boot (root): `echo 396000000 > /sys/class/devfreq/ff610000.dmc-cros/userspace/set_freq`.

## Kernel timers
- The device tree enables `timer@ff6b0000` (`rockchip,rk3288-timer`) as tick broadcast device, so the kernel runs high-resolution timers. SurfaceFlinger `missedFrames` per 72 s test went from 423-733 to 0-44.
- Check (root): `grep -m1 -A1 "^Broadcast device" /proc/timer_list` shows `rk_timer`.

## Display composition (HWC)
The HWC is the patched Rockchip HWC1 (`hwc/src-a16`), loaded through `hwc2on1adapter`.
- Landscape: the 2048x2048 wallpaper is rotated once by RGA and shown on a VOP window; the RGA result is reused while the wallpaper is unchanged. App drawer janky frames 68-71% -> 44-47%.
- More layers than VOP windows: unchanged bottom layers go into the client target, so SurfaceFlinger reuses its composition. Landscape shade janky frames 72-74% -> 47-50%.
- Status bar and task bar share one VOP window; every layer added to a window group is checked against every layer of the group for overlapping scanlines.
- `vendor/build.prop` sets `ro.surface_flinger.max_frame_buffer_acquired_buffers=3`. Recents opened from an app: janky frames 78-84% -> 43-47%.

## Charging
- Powered off on the charger the tablet charges with the panel dark.
- `persist.vendor.chg.boost=1` raises the adapter input current limit to 2 A (`vendor.chgpolicy`).

## Known issues
- Rare hard hang. The release kernel runs with `rkwdt=0` and writes a kernel log dump to the `kpanic` partition (`scripts/parse-logdump.py` reads it). The kernel command line `rkwdt=1 rkwdt_loader=1 rkwdt_sgrf=0x00420000` arms the hardware watchdog, which resets a hung tablet in 10-16 s and returns it to Maskrom.
